<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Hibis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Hibis is an Egyptian temple in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharga-oasis">Kharga Oasis</a> of the Western Desert, dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. Begun under the Saite kings, it was largely built and decorated under the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, making it the best-preserved temple of the Achaemenid period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Further additions were made under the Thirtieth Dynasty and the Ptolemies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-hibis.jpg" alt="Temple of Hibis" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RolandUnger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HibisComplete.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple stands about 2 kilometres north of Kharga and was once surrounded by the town of Hibis, in Egyptian Hebet, the plough, whose remains now lie under the fields. Archaeological evidence suggests an older temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> on the same spot. Building began in the 26th Dynasty, most likely under Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>, or possibly as early as the 25th Dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> is credited with the decoration of the walls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakor">Hakor</a> of the 29th Dynasty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, possibly Ptolemy IV and at least one Roman emperor added to it later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>It was devoted to two local forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Amun of Hibis and Amun-Ra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> who dwells in Hibis, though some hold it was dedicated to Amun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. An avenue of sphinxes passes through a series of pylons to the temple proper, which was once surrounded by a lake. The walls of the hypostyle hall are shaped like huge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> rolls and carry hymns to Amun, many known from earlier times, and a scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> defeating Apep that some art historians see as a forerunner of Saint George and the Dragon.</p><p>The walls are given to Theban theology and the roof to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>. The naos is divided into nine registers holding almost 700 figures of gods and kings, each register opening with the king performing a ritual, and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> appear in Osirian form, while the inscriptions beside them are brief or absent. The temple follows Theban temples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and Ptolemaic period in plan and texts, but its decoration is bolder in style.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Metropolitan Museum of Art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> led a first excavation that began in 1909, and a newer campaign led by Eugene Cruz-Uribe began in 1985. The temple's seclusion probably accounts for its good condition, but for several decades rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> has been damaging its foundations, and the Egyptian Antiquities Service considered taking the whole temple apart and moving it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple of Hibis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Darius I had Egyptian laws collected, completed the Nile to Red Sea canal and built the temple of <b>Hibis</b> in the Kharga Oasis.</blockquote><a href="/state/achaemenid-egypt">Achaemenid Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
